From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 06:51:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 051/342] mmc: large transfers, eMMC DDR52 and SD high speed on
 the Meson GX

Writes reached the card 4 to 16 KiB at a time: the controller driver
allowed one contiguous DMA segment per transfer, and the DMA resource's
bounce buffers hold 16 KiB. SD cards write such pieces very slowly
(1.3 MB/s on an ODROID-C2's card).

- meson_gx_mmc: an I/O operation of up to 64 scattered segments is
  gathered into (or, reading, scattered from) a 256 KiB bounce buffer,
  so one command moves up to 511 blocks; a single contiguous buffer
  still goes straight to DMA.
- eMMC DDR52: mmc_disk switches the card's BUS_WIDTH to 8-bit DDR when
  EXT_CSD's DEVICE_TYPE says so, then the host through a new optional
  set_ddr() of the bus and device interfaces. meson_gx_mmc runs its clock
  at twice the bus clock and finds the data resampling step (the ADJUST
  register) with CRC-checked test reads, keeping the middle of the
  passing window; without one, both go back to single data rate.
  "emmc_ddr false" in the meson_gx_mmc driver settings turns it off.
- SD high speed: after the 4-bit switch, CMD6 selects high speed and the
  clock goes to 50 MHz, after three test reads at it; else 25 MHz.
  meson_gx_mmc's short reads take fewer than 512 bytes for CMD6's status.

ODROID-C2, BFS on the eMMC (tools/probes/fsbench.c of the port):
sequential write 34.7 -> 62.4 MB/s, raw read 45.9 -> 75.5 MB/s, 2000
files of 8 KiB 569 -> 1075 files/s. DDR52 resampling passed at 8 of 10
steps on every boot. 480 MB in 7994 files written in DDR mode, and data
written before at single data rate, verified after a reboot. The SD card
reads at 22.2 instead of 11.9 MB/s, and its U-Boot read at 50 MHz equals
the image written to it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
